(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Objective: To assess whether curcumin supplementation provides renal protective benefits) Tj
T*
(beyond standard medical management. Setting: Veterinary nephrology clinic. Subjects: 16) Tj
T*
(cats with IRIS stage 3 chronic kidney disease randomly assigned to medical management plus) Tj
T*
(curcumin \(n=8\) or medical management alone \(n=8\). Interventions: 80 mg curcumin daily in) Tj
T*
(divided doses for 60 days. Outcomes: Baseline renal parameters were equivalent between) Tj
T*
(groups. The primary endpoint, serum symmetric dimethylarginine, declined more) Tj
T*
(substantially in the curcumin-supplemented group compared to standard treatment alone at) Tj
T*
(days 30, 45, and 60 \(p<0.01 for all timepoints\). Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio also) Tj
T*
(showed greater improvement over time in the supplemented group, with significant between-) Tj
T*
(group differences at later study visits. Antioxidant capacity increased while oxidative) Tj
T*
(stress markers decreased in the curcumin group. Inflammatory markers and apoptosis markers) Tj
T*
(showed trends consistent with reduced kidney cellular injury. All cats tolerated) Tj
T*
(supplementation well without adverse events. Conclusions: Curcumin co-treatment appeared) Tj
T*
(to slow renal biomarker deterioration in cats with stage 3 chronic kidney disease.) Tj
